Native v1.11 block-codec regressions
==================================

Run from this directory using a native Clang with sanitizer runtimes:

  python3 test_codec.py --cc clang --ubsan --asan \
    --upstream /path/to/lz4-c72c7c0ec132ad2ce2fe9fad7b0c728d9faee2fc \
    --compat-upstream /path/to/lz4-1.10.0

--compat-upstream is optional. Both trees are read-only inputs. Obtain them
independently from the upstream lz4/lz4 repository; the test validates hashes
of every source/header used, so another release is not silently substituted.
The harness downloads nothing and writes nothing to the kernel source tree.
--output /path/outside/the/source/tree retains artifacts; otherwise they are
removed automatically. --seed changes the deterministic upstream test seed.
Dependencies also include the Python packages listed below for the shared
header shims/ELF reader. This is a Linux host test (dlopen and UBSan SIGILL).

The real three kernel objects are compiled separately, not replaced by an
upstream userspace library. Upstream v1.11's block/HC unit framework supplies
70 applicable regressions. Four additional tests check kernel sizes, poisoned
first-use HC reset and scratch/canaries, exact legacy HC level normalization,
the real STATIC preboot wrapper, and bidirectional cross-decoding. Test-only
adapters allocate caller workspaces outside the codec. Userspace allocator,
obsolete-API and optional-extension-contract tests are printed as OMITTED;
none are silently counted as successes. The supported alignment checks are
retained without obsolete reset assertions. No frame/CLI/thread tests run.
Reference libraries use local symbol binding (-Bsymbolic) to avoid accidentally
calling the kernel codec instead of the intended reference implementation.

ASan references must be present in all three objects, and an intentional
signed-overflow probe must actually trap when --ubsan is selected. --asan
requires a working ASan compiler/runtime. Zig 0.14.1's "cc" driver is not a
substitute: combined flags can lose ASan instrumentation, and ASan-only
linking can lack its runtime. Missing instrumentation or link failures are
not counted as sanitized passes. Its UBSan trap mode can be used with
--ubsan alone. The local ASan run used raw Clang 19.1.7 with the host
libasan.so.8, plus UBSan traps; that is a host test, not an AOSP kernel build.


ARM64 LZ4 standalone regression test
==================================

This optional developer test cross-compiles the real LZ4 sources and emulates
AArch64/NEON instructions. It does NOT require an ARM64 host or device, and it
does not change the kernel configuration. It is deliberately not registered
in the normal native selftest run because it needs an emulator and a cross
compiler/linker.

Requirements:
  * Python >= 3.9 (run without -O; assertions are test checks)
  * pip packages unicorn==2.1.4 and pyelftools==0.32
  * Clang and LLD with AArch64, SCS and ThinLTO support

Example using a separately installed toolchain:
  python3 test_arm64.py --cc /path/to/clang

An alternative for freestanding checks only (NOT the release compiler) is
Zig 0.14.1's Clang 19.1.7, available as the ziglang==0.14.1 pip package:
  python3 test_arm64.py --cc '/path/to/zig cc'

By default artifacts live in an automatically removed temporary directory.
Use --output /path/outside/the/source/tree to retain objects and ELF files.
The --cc argument also accepts the CC environment variable. No GCC is used.

Two executables are tested: ordinary Clang and SCS+ThinLTO. The actual .S
file and C dispatch are used; only kernel headers, SIMD context protection,
CPU-part/FPSIMD queries, and memory primitives are replaced by small shims.
Memory-access hooks enforce input/output bounds and read-only compressed
input for nonoverlapping decoding. Callee-saved registers and x18 are checked.
The SCS executable must exercise shadow-stack writes, not merely accept a
compiler flag.

Coverage includes valid and malformed blocks, full/partial output, all short
match offsets, long lengths, unaligned buffers, multi-sequence blocks, C-tail
matches into the SIMD prefix, forbidden SIMD/FPSIMD-unavailable fallbacks,
DIP/overlapping-buffer fallbacks, small buffers, signed/size_t size guards,
and first/prefix/external-dictionary streaming blocks. Error offsets need
not be identical. Assembly rejects zero offsets even where upstream C may
accept them; any accepted random malformed block must match C's output.

The build also checks that STATIC, feature-off, and no-NEON objects do not
reference SIMD. It does not validate real kernel preemption, FPSIMD ownership,
module loading, the complete preboot wrapper, device boot or performance.
A full AOSP Clang r614150 kernel build and device tests remain necessary.
CONFIG_LZ4_ARM64_ACCEL is experimental and defaults to n.

The v1.11 follow-up also compiles all three C objects for ARM32, audits their
allocation-free external symbols and the unchanged 16 kernel exports, and
runs 27 real ARM64 default/fast/HC compression roundtrips with output and
workspace canaries (including levels 10--12 and legacy 16). These compression
checks run in the ordinary executable; SCS+ThinLTO remains a decoder-path
check. No new device, FPSIMD/preemption or performance validation is implied.
